\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental-setup}

\paragraph{Models.}
\label{sec:models}
We evaluate on two 7B-parameter models sharing the Qwen2 backbone (28 layers, 28 heads, 4-group GQA): \textbf{DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B}~\cite{deepseek-r1-2025}, distilled from DeepSeek-R1 with explicit chain-of-thought traces yielding pronounced phase structure; and \textbf{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct}~\cite{qwen25-2024}, with standard instruction tuning, providing a cross-model replication target. Both are loaded in \texttt{bfloat16} with eager attention.\footnote{An earlier \texttt{float16} configuration produced sporadic NaN values; \texttt{bfloat16} eliminated all occurrences.}

\paragraph{Dataset.}
\label{sec:dataset}
We use 50 problems stratified from MATH-500~\cite{math500-neurips2021}, sampling uniformly across categories with a fixed seed. Each problem is decoded greedily (\texttt{max\_new\_tokens}$=$2048).

\paragraph{Sparsity configuration.}
\label{sec:sparsity-config}
We apply symmetric compound sparsity at $s \in \{0.15, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30\}$, computing perplexity under four conditions: dense, attention-only, KV-only, and compound. We compare \emph{importance-based} eviction (position-normalized cumulative attention score, Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance}; BOS always retained) and \emph{random} eviction (uniform). Heads are pruned per layer by Frobenius norm; under random eviction, both heads and positions are selected uniformly.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation and metrics.}
\label{sec:phase-seg}
Each sequence is segmented into three phases using the quantile-based method (\S\ref{sec:phase-segmentation}), with $\texttt{min\_phase\_length}{=}10$. We compute segment-level perplexity, II (Eq.~\ref{eq:ii}), and $G$ (Eq.~\ref{eq:gradient}), reporting medians due to heavy right-skew (mean/median ratios up to 43:1).

\paragraph{Statistical analysis.}
\label{sec:stats}
All confidence intervals and significance tests use nonparametric bootstrap ($B{=}10{,}000$)~\cite{efron-1993}. Paired bootstrap controls for problem-level variance. All tests yield $p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$ at every sparsity level.\footnote{Experiments on 4$\times$ NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell 96\,GB GPUs.}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Strategy-Dependent Severity}
\label{sec:strategy-severity}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregate-ii} reports the median compound severity difference across strategies and sparsity levels. Under importance-based eviction, $\Delta$ increases from $0.39$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $14.59$ at $s{=}0.30$---a $37{\times}$ amplification. Under random eviction, no systematic growth is observed ($-0.02$ to $1.54$), confirming that compound severity depends qualitatively on eviction strategy. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Median compound severity difference ($\Delta = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} - \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$) by eviction strategy and sparsity level on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n{=}50$ MATH-500 problems).}
\label{tab:aggregate-ii}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & $s{=}0.15$ & $s{=}0.20$ & $s{=}0.25$ & $s{=}0.30$ \\
\midrule
Importance & 0.39 & 1.48 & 7.16 & 14.59 \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json median_diff
Random     & 0.01 & $-$0.02 & 0.54 & 1.54 \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json median_diff
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Phase-Modulated Amplification}
\label{sec:phase-amplification}

Decomposing II by reasoning phase reveals that strategy-dependent severity is driven by amplification in the low-sparsity phase. Table~\ref{tab:main-results} reports the full per-phase breakdown. The severity gradient $G$ increases from $69{\times}$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $274{\times}$ at $s{=}0.30$ under importance-based eviction, while the high-sparsity phase experiences near-zero interaction ($\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ ranges from $0.006$ to $0.053$). Under random eviction, $G \approx 0.9$--$1.7{\times}$ at all sparsity levels. Figure~\ref{fig:hero} visualizes this contrast. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Interaction index (II) by eviction strategy, reasoning phase, and sparsity level. II values are problem-level medians across 50 problems. $\Delta = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} - \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$. $G = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} / \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$.}
\label{tab:main-results}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccr@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Strategy & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$ & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{mid}}$ & $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}}$ & $\Delta$ & $G$ \\
\midrule
0.15 & Importance & 0.006 & 0.081 & 0.398 & 0.39 & $\mathbf{69}$ \\
0.15 & Random     & 0.093 & 0.097 & 0.103 & 0.01 & 1.1 \\
\addlinespace
0.20 & Importance & 0.015 & 0.203 & 1.496 & 1.48 & $\mathbf{103}$ \\
0.20 & Random     & 0.292 & 0.350 & 0.276 & $-$0.02 & 0.9 \\
\addlinespace
0.25 & Importance & 0.032 & 1.118 & 7.196 & 7.16 & $\mathbf{222}$ \\
0.25 & Random     & 0.818 & 0.932 & 1.363 & 0.54 & 1.7 \\
\addlinespace
0.30 & Importance & 0.053 & 2.283 & 14.639 & 14.59 & $\mathbf{274}$ \\
0.30 & Random     & 2.503 & 2.592 & 4.041 & 1.54 & 1.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Severity gradient ($G$) as a function of sparsity level $s$. Importance-based eviction (solid) exhibits an increasing gradient from $69{\times}$ to $274{\times}$; random eviction (dashed) remains flat near $1{\times}$. Shaded regions: 95\% bootstrap CIs.} % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030
\label{fig:hero}
\end{figure}


\subsection{Phase-Strategy Crossover}
\label{sec:crossover}

A strategy ranking reversal emerges across all sparsity levels: at $s{=}0.25$, importance-based eviction yields \emph{lower} II than random in the high-sparsity phase ($0.032$ vs.\ $0.818$) but \emph{higher} II in the low-sparsity phase ($7.196$ vs.\ $1.363$---a $5.3{\times}$ reversal), demonstrating that no single strategy dominates uniformly. Full crossover analysis and visualization appear in Appendix~\ref{app:crossover-details}. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s025, bf16_ds_rand_s025; source: experiment_summary_table.json; 7.196/1.363=5.28

\subsection{Cross-Model Replication}
\label{sec:cross-model}

Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (standard instruction tuning, same Qwen2 backbone) reproduces the monotonic severity gradient: $\Delta$ increases from $0.04$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $2.94$ at $s{=}0.30$, paralleling DeepSeek at ${\sim}1/5$ the magnitude. Both models yield strictly increasing $d_z$ trajectories (Spearman $\rho = 1.0$; gradient permutation $p = 0.043$). The effect is marginal at $s{=}0.15$ ($d_z{=}0.28$, CI includes zero) but reliable at $s \geq 0.20$ ($d_z \geq 0.55$). Full cross-model tables and figures appear in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-details}. % exp_id: cross_model_analysis.json; cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json


\subsection{Cross-Architecture Validation}
\label{sec:cross-arch}

To test whether the phase-modulated severity pattern extends beyond the Qwen2 architecture, we replicate the experiment on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM, 8B parameters; $n{=}49$ paired problems). Table~\ref{tab:llama-cross-arch} reports the strategy-dependent severity comparison across sparsity levels.

The qualitative pattern replicates: importance-based eviction produces systematically larger phase-dependent severity than random eviction, with monotonically increasing paired $d_z$ ($0.19 \to 0.26 \to 0.29 \to 0.34$). At $s{=}0.30$, the Wilcoxon test reaches significance after Bonferroni correction ($p = 0.029$, $k{=}4$). At $s{=}0.15$, the median difference CI $[0.002, 0.043]$ excludes zero despite the small absolute magnitude ($\widetilde{\Delta} = 0.020$). Under importance-based eviction, the within-strategy phase gradient $d_z$ (low $-$ high) is consistently large at all sparsity levels ($d_z \in [1.00, 1.12]$), while random eviction yields only small phase gradients ($d_z \in [0.35, 0.45]$). % exp_id: c3_llama

The absolute magnitude is architecture-dependent: LLaMA severity differences are ${\sim}40{\times}$ smaller than DeepSeek and ${\sim}7{\times}$ smaller than Qwen, and the interaction is substantially weaker---random pruning also shows a non-trivial phase gradient on LLaMA ($d_z \in [0.35, 0.45]$), reducing the strategy contrast observed in Qwen2 models. The directional pattern---monotonically increasing $d_z$, importance $>$ random, larger phase gradient under importance---is qualitatively consistent across all three models spanning two architectures (Qwen2ForCausalLM, LlamaForCausalLM), though the interaction magnitude is substantially weaker on LlamaForCausalLM. % exp_id: c3_llama; cross_model_analysis.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-architecture validation on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (LlamaForCausalLM). $d_z$: paired Cohen's $d$ for importance vs.\ random severity; $\widetilde{\Delta}$: median paired difference with bootstrap 95\% CI ($B{=}10{,}000$); $p$: Bonferroni-corrected Wilcoxon $p$ ($k{=}4$). $n{=}49$ paired problems.}
\label{tab:llama-cross-arch}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & $d_z$ & $\widetilde{\Delta}$\; [95\% CI] & $p_{\text{Bonf}}$ \\
\midrule
0.15 & 0.19 & 0.020\; $[0.002, 0.043]$ & 0.219 \\
0.20 & 0.26 & 0.022\; $[-0.090, 0.187]$ & 0.253 \\
0.25 & 0.29 & 0.095\; $[-0.004, 0.303]$ & 0.083 \\
0.30 & 0.34 & 0.356\; $[-0.069, 0.544]$ & $\mathbf{0.029}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Statistical Validation}
\label{sec:stat-validation}

Paired permutation tests ($10{,}000$ permutations, Bonferroni-corrected) confirm that importance-based eviction produces significantly larger phase-dependent severity than random eviction at all four sparsity levels ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$), with observed median differences increasing monotonically from $0.37$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $9.85$ at $s{=}0.30$. Full permutation test results and forest plot appear in Appendix~\ref{app:permutation-details}. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; source: permutation_test_results.json

Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d} reports paired Cohen's $d_z$ for both models. For DeepSeek, effect sizes grow from $d_z{=}0.44$ (small) to $d_z{=}0.80$ (large). For Qwen, the effect is marginal at $s{=}0.15$ ($d_z{=}0.28$, CI includes zero), becoming reliable at $s \geq 0.20$ ($d_z \geq 0.55$). At $s \geq 0.25$, Qwen exhibits larger standardized effect sizes ($d_z{=}0.89$/$1.02$ vs.\ $0.68$/$0.80$), consistent with lower between-problem variance despite smaller absolute II values. % exp_id: cohens_d_bf16.json; cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Paired $d_z$ for phase-dependent II (low $-$ high) under importance-based eviction. Bootstrap 95\% CIs ($B{=}10{,}000$).}
\label{tab:cohens-d}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & DS $d_z$ & $\widetilde{\Delta}$ [CI] & QW $d_z$ & $\widetilde{\Delta}$ [CI] \\
\midrule
0.15 & 0.44 & 0.32\; $[0.20, 0.47]$ & 0.28 & 0.05\; $[-0.01, 0.11]$\textsuperscript{ns} \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s015, bf16_qw_imp_s015
0.20 & 0.52 & 1.15\; $[0.66, 1.26]$ & 0.55 & 0.21\; $[0.07, 0.40]$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s020, bf16_qw_imp_s020
0.25 & 0.68 & 3.97\; $[2.83, 5.29]$ & 0.89 & 1.11\; $[0.61, 1.47]$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s025, bf16_qw_imp_s025
0.30 & 0.80 & 9.94\; $[8.39, 16.65]$ & 1.02 & 2.62\; $[1.72, 3.65]$ \\ % exp_id: bf16_ds_imp_s030, bf16_qw_imp_s030
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{@{}l}{\footnotesize \textsuperscript{ns}Marginal at $s{=}0.15$; reliable at $s \geq 0.20$.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

We provide a mechanistic analysis in terms of entropy-discrimination collapse in \S\ref{sec:analysis}.

\section{Analysis: Entropy-Discrimination Collapse}
\label{sec:analysis}

We propose \emph{discrimination collapse} as the primary mechanism driving the phase-strategy interaction. Head pruning reduces the number of heads contributing to cumulative importance scores, flattening the score distribution: the Gini coefficient decreases by $0.030$--$0.081$ (DeepSeek) and $0.010$--$0.020$ (Qwen) across all sparsity levels, with consistent negative deltas in all 16 conditions (Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:discrimination-metrics-full}). When importance scores lose discriminability, thresholding degenerates into noise-sensitive selection---functionally approaching biased random eviction. This effect is phase-dependent: in low-sparsity phases, attention is already broadly distributed, so pruning-induced flattening has maximum impact; in high-sparsity phases, concentration is preserved. Random eviction is immune because its decisions are independent of score quality, explaining $G \approx 1$. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen

Beyond discrimination collapse, head pruning redistributes the attention entropy of surviving heads. For DeepSeek, the entropy delta is consistently negative ($\Delta\mathcal{H} \in [-0.047, -0.040]$), indicating that importance-based pruning systematically selects heads whose removal concentrates attention in survivors. For Qwen, the pattern partially replicates at $s \leq 0.20$ but reverses at $s{=}0.25$ ($\Delta\mathcal{H} = +0.045$), suggesting model-specific head specialization patterns (Appendix~\ref{app:analysis}, Table~\ref{tab:entropy-deltas}). We characterize discrimination collapse as the robust, model-independent driver, with entropy redistribution as a secondary, model-dependent amplifier. % exp_id: c4_entropy_deepseek, c4_entropy_qwen

