% Section 3: Background and Problem Setup
% Note: eq:ii and eq:gradient are the canonical definitions.
% Full formulation details in Appendix (app:formulation-details).

\section{Background and Problem Setup}
\label{sec:background}

We formalize compound sparsity, define the Interaction Index, and introduce phase segmentation. Full mathematical details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

\paragraph{Compound sparsity.}
\label{sec:compound-sparsity}
Let $s \in (0,1)$ denote a unified sparsity budget. \emph{Attention head pruning} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of heads per layer by Frobenius norm importance \cite{voita-acl2019, michel-neurips2019}. \emph{KV cache eviction} removes the bottom-$s$ fraction of token positions from the cache. We compare two strategies: \emph{importance-based}, which scores positions by position-normalized cumulative attention mass (Eq.~\ref{eq:kv-importance} in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}), and \emph{random}, which evicts uniformly at random \cite{nacl-acl2024}. Applying both simultaneously yields \emph{compound sparsity}; the two axes interact because head pruning alters the importance ranking used for eviction \cite{spark-aaai2026}. Existing joint-pruning approaches such as GRASPrune \cite{graspune-acl2026} learn global budget-constrained gates but treat decisions as static across generation.

\paragraph{Interaction Index.}
\label{sec:interaction-index}
For a text segment, let $\text{PPL}_c$ denote perplexity under condition $c \in \{d, a, k, b\}$ (dense, attention-only, KV-only, compound). Normalizing by the dense baseline yields $r_c = \text{PPL}_c / \text{PPL}_d$, and:
\begin{equation}
    \mathrm{II} = r_b - r_a - r_k + 1.
    \label{eq:ii}
\end{equation}
$\mathrm{II} > 0$ indicates super-additive degradation; $\mathrm{II} = 0$ indicates additive independence. We adopt the additive null because the two axes operate on disjoint dimensions; qualitative conclusions hold under the multiplicative null (Appendix~\ref{app:multiplicative-null}). Because the II distribution is heavily right-skewed, we report medians throughout.

\paragraph{Severity gradient.}
To capture phase variation:
\begin{equation}
    G = \frac{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{low}}}{\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{high}}},
    \label{eq:gradient}
\end{equation}
where $\widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\,\textit{low/high}}$ are median II in the low/high-sparsity phases. $G \gg 1$ indicates phase-concentrated severity.

\paragraph{Phase segmentation.}
\label{sec:phase-segmentation}
Each generated sequence is partitioned into three phases (low-, mid-, high-sparsity) by tercile boundaries of per-position attention sparsity, following ThinKV~\cite{thinkv-iclr2026}. Low-sparsity phases correspond to broadly distributed attention (exploratory reasoning); high-sparsity phases to concentrated attention (structured computation). Details in Appendix~\ref{app:formulation-details}.

% Content moved from main body to appendix:
% - Phase-strategy crossover details (from results §5.3)
% - Cross-model replication details with tables/figures (from results §5.4)
% - Permutation test details with table/forest plot (from results §5.5)
% - Full entropy-discrimination collapse analysis with tables (from analysis §6)
% Main body §6 (analysis.tex) contains a condensed summary referencing these tables.

\section{Phase-Strategy Crossover Details}
\label{app:crossover-details}

At $s{=}0.25$, a strategy ranking reversal emerges (Figure~\ref{fig:crossover}): importance-based eviction yields \emph{lower} II than random in the high-sparsity phase ($0.032$ vs.\ $0.818$) but \emph{higher} II in the low-sparsity phase ($7.196$ vs.\ $1.363$---a $5.3{\times}$ reversal). This crossover is present at all four sparsity levels, with reversal magnitude of $3.9{\times}$ at $s{=}0.15$, $5.4{\times}$ at $s{=}0.20$, $5.3{\times}$ at $s{=}0.25$, and $3.6{\times}$ at $s{=}0.30$ (as random eviction severity also rises). The reversal arises because head pruning redistributes attention mass, altering importance rankings in a way that couples the two sparsity mechanisms in low-sparsity phases (\S\ref{sec:discrimination-collapse}). % exp_id: bf16_importance_s025, bf16_ds_rand_s025; source: experiment_summary_table.json; 7.196/1.363=5.28

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_crossover.pdf}
\caption{Strategy-dependent crossover at $s{=}0.25$. Grouped bar chart of median II by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. Importance-based eviction outperforms random in the high-sparsity phase but produces $5.3{\times}$ higher II in the low-sparsity phase, reversing the strategy ranking.}
\label{fig:crossover}
\end{figure}


\section{Cross-Model Replication Details}
\label{app:cross-model-details}

Both models share the Qwen2ForCausalLM backbone (28 layers, 28 heads, 4-group GQA) but differ in training: DeepSeek uses reasoning distillation from DeepSeek-R1, while Qwen2.5 uses standard instruction tuning without explicit reasoning delimiters. This controlled comparison isolates the effect of training methodology on the compound sparsity interaction.

Despite lacking explicit \texttt{<think>} delimiters, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct exhibits sufficient variation in attention sparsity to permit phase segmentation via the same quantile-based method (\S\ref{sec:phase-segmentation}). Four of the 50 problems are excluded due to degenerate phase assignments, yielding $n{=}46$ valid problems for Qwen (vs.\ $n{=}49$ for DeepSeek).

Table~\ref{tab:cross-model} reports the cross-model comparison. Qwen reproduces the monotonic severity gradient at ${\sim}1/5$ of DeepSeek's magnitude. Figure~\ref{fig:cross-model} visualizes this comparison. % exp_id: cross_model_analysis.json; cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model comparison of median compound severity difference ($\Delta$) under importance-based eviction.}
\label{tab:cross-model}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & $s{=}0.15$ & $s{=}0.20$ & $s{=}0.25$ & $s{=}0.30$ \\
